    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate leaning heavily on **Pete Crow-Armstrong**, whose projection of 82.13 makes him our clear offensive anchor. His position at the top of the order combined with the favorable matchup against the right-handed L\u00f3pez provides an elite ceiling, bolstered by a strong 0.371 xwOBA. **Lars Nootbaar** also earns \"Strong\" status (67.77) for similar reasons; batting leadoff against a mid-tier arm like Alcantara offers him the plate appearances and platoon advantage necessary to generate the run-scoring opportunities we covet. These two are the primary engines for our Roto categories today, particularly in stolen base potential and table-setting for the middle-order hitters.\n\nOur lineup construction remains defensive against high-end talent, as evidenced by our decision to bench **Chase Meidroth** (35.32) and **Hector Rodriguez** (34.98). While both carry decent base projections, the combination of facing elite arms like Williams and Skubal\u2014whose sub-3.00 xERA profiles significantly cap their potential output\u2014pushed them below our 40-point floor threshold. **Chase Meidroth** was the closest to a start, but the high-strikeout threat posed by Williams ultimately rendered him a liability given our strict focus on daily efficiency. We are also monitoring the remaining lineup slots; **Dillon Dingler** (43.63), **Kazuma Okamoto** (55.16), **Josh Smith** (55.40), **Pedro Ramirez** (49.02), **Christian Encarnacion-Strand** (55.27), and **Carson Benge** (57.53) represent a \"marginal to strong\" block. **Josh Smith** benefits from a dynamic platoon boost of +14%, though we caution that his low spot in the order slightly dampens his overall scoring trajectory compared to our leadoff hitters. With **Yordan Alvarez** and **Junior Caminero** sidelined by team off-days, we are counting on this group to maintain our standing without forcing low-value plate appearances against high-efficiency pitching.",
